                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Present continuous",
                    "explanation": "El present continuous describe lo que está pasando ahora: It's raining, I'm wearing a jumper. Se forma con am / is / are + verbo en -ing.\n\nNegativa: I'm not smiling! Pregunta: What are you doing? Are you smiling?\n\nCompáralo con el present simple, que es para rutinas: Sam always sleeps on Saturdays (rutina) / Sam is sleeping now (ahora). Pistas: now, right now, at the moment, Look! → continuous; always, usually, every day → simple.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Sujeto",
                        "Afirmativa",
                        "Negativa",
                        "Pregunta"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I",
                          "I'm working",
                          "I'm not working",
                          "Am I working?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "you / we / they",
                          "you're working",
                          "you aren't working",
                          "Are you working?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "he / she / it",
                          "she's working",
                          "she isn't working",
                          "Is she working?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "It's raining again.",
                      "Priya is working today.",
                      "What are you doing? — I'm looking at the rain.",
                      "Sam always sleeps on Saturdays, and he's sleeping now."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "be going to: plans",
                    "explanation": "Be going to + verbo sirve para hablar de planes e intenciones: I'm going to watch a film this afternoon (voy a ver una película).\n\nSe forma con am / is / are + going to + verbo: She's going to visit her family. Negativa: I'm not going to go out. Pregunta: What are you going to do? Are you going to stay at home?\n\nSuele ir con expresiones de futuro: this afternoon, tonight, tomorrow, next week, at the weekend.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Afirmativa",
                          "I'm going to watch a film tonight."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "We aren't going to go to the park."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "What are you going to do tomorrow?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Respuesta corta",
                          "Are you going to stay at home? — Yes, I am. / No, I'm not."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "It's raining. I'm going to stay at home.",
                      "Sam is going to play his guitar this afternoon.",
                      "What are you going to do tomorrow?",
                      "We aren't going to go to the park today."
                    ]
                  }
                },
